      "latency_ms": 1826.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 769,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. The transitions are: melting (0 °C), boiling (100 °C at 1 atm), sublimation, and deposition. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature rises, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” that allows insects to walk on water and plants to transport water upward (capillary action).<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in cold climates. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of oceans, 2 % of freshwater lakes/rivers, 1 % ice caps/glaciers). Also present in the atmosphere as water vapor, in soils, and within living organisms (≈ 60 % of the human body by weight). |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and DNA replication all occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Transport** – blood, sap, and cellular fluids move nutrients, waste, and gases.<br>• **Structural** – provides turgor pressure in plant cells, maintains cell shape.<br>• **Thermoregulation** – sweating, panting, and evaporative cooling rely on water’s high heat of vaporization. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | • Drinking and cooking<br>• Agriculture (irrigation)<br>• Power generation (hydropower, steam turbines)<br>• Manufacturing (solvent, coolant, cleaning)<br>• Transportation (ships, barges)<br>• Recreation (swimming, sports) |\n| **Environmental importance** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – water continuously moves between oceans, atmosphere, land, and living organisms via evaporation, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, and runoff.<br>• **Climate regulation** – oceans store and redistribute heat; water vapor is a potent greenhouse gas.<br>• **Ecosystem support** – freshwater habitats (rivers, lakes, wetlands) host diverse flora and fauna. |\n\n### In a nutshell\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) that can exist as ice, liquid, or vapor under Earth’s conditions. Its unique physical and chemical properties—especially its ability to dissolve many substances, its high heat capacity, and its density anomaly—make it the cornerstone of Earth’s climate, geology, and all known biological processes. Without water, the chemistry of life as we know it could not occur."
